{%- doc -%}
  Works out which badge a product wears, from its tags.

  The badges themselves are five slots in Theme settings under Product badges —
  a match title and a colour each — so a shop runs its badges from one place
  rather than from a field on every product. Tagging is something a merchant
  already does, and often already has: the products here arrive carrying
  "Custom Treats" and "choose your own" in whatever casing the day called for,
  which is why the match ignores case.

  The first filled slot that matches wins, so the order of the slots is the
  order of precedence: a product tagged both Bestsellers and New shows whichever
  sits higher. The title in the slot is what the card prints, not the tag as the
  product spells it, so "choose your own" still reads "Choose your own".

  Outputs `Title|#colour`, or nothing when no slot matches. Liquid has no way to
  hand back two values, and a snippet that rendered the badge itself would put
  the card's own positioning outside the card; so the caller splits on the pipe,
  in two lines.

  @param {object} product - The product to read tags from.

  @example
  {%- capture badge -%}{% render 'ui-product-badge', product: product %}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- assign badge_parts = badge | strip | split: '|' -%}
  {% render 'ui-product-card', badge: badge_parts[0], badge_color: badge_parts[1] %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign found = ''
  assign slots = '1,2,3,4,5' | split: ','

  for slot in slots
    if found != ''
      break
    endif

    assign title_key = 'badge_' | append: slot | append: '_title'
    assign color_key = 'badge_' | append: slot | append: '_color'

    assign slot_title = settings[title_key]
    if slot_title == blank
      continue
    endif

    assign wanted = slot_title | strip | downcase

    for tag in product.tags
      assign candidate = tag | strip | downcase
      if candidate == wanted
        assign found = slot_title | append: '|' | append: settings[color_key]
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endfor

  echo found
-%}
